The cannonball is fired from the left and follows a parabolic trajectory. In the first frame (t=0.00), it is aimed at the robot labeled 1, which is on the ground. The subsequent frames show the cannonball moving rightward and downward, approaching robot 1. By t=0.27, it is clearly above robot 1 and descending toward it. At t=0.37, it is still descending toward robot 1, which is now directly below its path.

Since the cannonball is moving downward and the robot is on the ground, the cannonball will hit robot 1. The impact will be strong enough to destroy robot 1, as it is the only robot in the path and no other object is in the way to absorb the impact. Robots 2 and 3 are not in the cannonball's path and will remain untouched. Therefore, robots 2 and 3 will survive.

